{{smaller|'''Red wash.''' (''a'') A lotion composed of corrosive sublimate red sulphid of mercury, and creosote, in water. (''b'') Bates’ camphorated water, made by adding copper sulphate Armenian bole, and camphor to boiling water, and the straining.—'''Tooth-wash,''' a liquid dentifrice.—'''Whit wash,''' Goulard’s lotion; lead-water.—'''Yellow wash,''' lotion prepared by dissolving 30 grains of corrosive sublimate in one pint of lime-water.}}

¶
'''wash†''' (wosh), ''a.'' [< ''wash'', ''v.'' (cf. ''washy''); perhap < *''warsh'' for ''wearish''.] Washy; weak; easil losing its qualities.
{{smaller block|Faith, ’tis but a ''wash'' scent.
{{right|''Marston'', What you Will, i. 1}}
Their bodies of so weak and ''wash'' a temper.
{{right|''Fletcher'', Bonduca, iv. 1}}
’Tis a ''wash'' knave; he will not keep his flesh well.
{{right|''Fletcher'', Rule a Wife, iii. 1}}}}

'''washable''' (wosh′ạ-bl), ''a.'' [< ''wash'' + ''-able''.] Resisting or enduring washing: noting the fabric and also the color.
{{smaller block|Like ''washable'' beaver hats that improve with rain, h nerves were rendered stouter and more vigorous b showers of tears.
{{right|''Dickens'', Oliver Twist, xxxvi}}}}

'''wash-back''' (wosh′bak), ''n.'' In ''distilling'', a cistern or vat in which the wort is fermented t form the wash. ''E. H. Knight.''

'''wash-ball''' (wosh′bâl), ''n.'' A ball of soap some times combined with cosmetics.
{{smaller block|We furnish’d ourselves with ''wash-balls'', the best bein made here, and being a considerable commodity.
{{right|''Evelyn'', Diary, May 21, 164}}}}

readings.c2s3:
¶
{{smaller block|I had worked myself up, as I always do, in the manner of heavy men; growing hot like an ill-''washered'' wheel revolving, though I start with a cool axle.
{{right|''R. D. Blackmore'', Lorna Doone, lxx.}}
He ''washered'' the knobs of the doors that had a rattling play whenever handled.{{right|''Sci. Amer.'', N. S., LV. 160.}}}}

'''washer-cutter''' (wosh′ėr-kut″ėr), ''n.'' A rotating cutting-tool with two adjustable cutters, worked by a hand-brace or by a drill, and used for cutting out annular disks for washers. ''E. H. Knight.''

'''washer-gage''' (wosh′ėr-gāj), ''n.'' A graduated tapering rule used for measuring the diameter of bolts, nuts, and washers, and of holes, etc., to receive them.

'''washer-hoop''' (wosh′ėr-höp), ''n.'' In a water-wheel, a gasket placed between the flange and the curb. ''E. H. Knight.''

'''washerman''' (wosh′ėr-man), ''n.''; pl. ''washermen'' (-men). A man who washes clothes, etc.—'''Washermen’s itch.''' {{smaller|Same as ''dhobies’ itch'' (which see).}}

readings.c3s1:
¶
'''wash-house''' (wosh′hous), ''n.'' [ME. *''waschhous'', < AS. ''wæsc-hus'', < ''wascan'', wash, + ''hūs'', house; as ''wash'' + ''house''<sup>1</sup>.] A house, generally fitted with boilers, tubs, etc., for washing clothes, etc.; a washing-house.

'''washiness''' (wosh′i-nes), ''n.'' The state of being washy, watery, or worthless; want of strength.

'''washing''' (wosh′ing), ''n.'' [< ME. ''wasshinge'', ''waschynge'', ''wessinge'', ''waschunge'', < AS. ''wæscing'', washing, verbal n. of ''wascan'', wash: see ''wash'', ''v.''] '''1.''' The act of cleansing with water; ablution. {{smaller|Ceremonial washing has been practised in ancient and modern times and among various peoples. The principal ceremonial washings in the modern Christian church are two: ''washing of feet'', in commemoration of the washing of the feet of the disciples by Christ (see ''foot''); and ''washing of the hands'', especially in connection with the celebration of the eucharist. In the Western Church, as well as in the Greek and other Oriental churches, the priest washes his hands before celebration. In the Western Church he also washes his fingers after the offertory and at the end of the eucharistic office. See ''ablution'', ''lavabo'', ''purification'', and ''holy water'' (under ''water'').}}
{{smaller block|John wondered why the Messias, the Lamb of God, pure and without spot, who needed not the abstersions of repentance, or the ''washings'' of baptism, should demand it.
{{right|''Jer. Taylor'', Works (ed. 1835), I. 95.}}}}
'''2.''' Clothes washed, especially those washed at one time; a wash.—'''3.''' The result of washing; that which is washed from something else, as gold dust.—{{smaller|'''To give one’s head for washing†''', to submit to insult.}}
{{smaller block|So am I, and forty more good fellows, that will not ''give their heads for the washing'', I take it.
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Cupid’s Revenge, iv. 3.}}}}

